1
00:04:49,565 --> 00:04:55,565
The untold story of the wild west.

2
00:09:39,130 --> 00:09:45,130
Posse is a action, adventure, western film directed by Mario Van Peebles.

3
00:14:28,695 --> 00:14:34,695
A group of mostly black infantrymen return from the
Spanish-American War with a cache of gold.

4
00:19:18,260 --> 00:19:24,260
They travel to the West where their leader searches
for the men who lynched his father.

5
00:24:07,826 --> 00:24:13,826
Jesse Lee, played by Mario Van Peebles.

6
00:28:57,391 --> 00:29:03,391
Jimmy J. 'Little J' Teeters, played by Stephen Baldwin.

7
00:33:46,956 --> 00:33:52,956
Obobo, played by Tommy Lister Jr..

8
00:38:36,521 --> 00:38:42,521
Father Time, played by Big Daddy Kane.

9
00:43:26,086 --> 00:43:32,086
Colonel Graham, played by Billy Zane.

10
00:48:15,652 --> 00:48:21,652
Phoebe, played by Pam Grier.

11
00:53:05,217 --> 00:53:11,217
Posse is a 1993 American  Western film directed by
and starring Mario Van Peebles.

12
00:57:54,782 --> 00:58:00,782
Featuring a large ensemble cast, the film tells the story of a posse of African-American
soldiers and one ostracized white soldier, who are all betrayed by a corrupt colonel.

13
01:02:44,347 --> 01:02:50,347
The story starts with the group escaping with a cache of gold, and continues with their leader Jesse
Lee (Mario Van Peebles) taking revenge on the men who killed his preacher father.

14
01:07:33,913 --> 01:07:39,913
The story is presented as a flashback told by an unnamed old man (Woody Strode).

15
01:12:23,478 --> 01:12:29,478
The title of the film refers to a group of people who are
summoned to help law enforcement officers.

16
01:17:13,043 --> 01:17:19,043
This film was the first to be released by Gramercy Pictures.

17
01:22:02,608 --> 01:22:08,608
Melvin Van Peebles, the father of director Mario Van Peebles,
recorded a new song, "Cruel Jim Crow", for the film.

18
01:26:52,173 --> 01:26:58,173
This marked his first music recording in 20 years, after his
1974 album What the....You Mean I Can't Sing?!

19
01:31:41,739 --> 01:31:47,739
It led to the production of a new album, Ghetto Gothic, released in 1995.

20
01:36:31,304 --> 01:36:37,304
The film takes the form of an extended memory, as told by Woody
Strode, who witnessed some of the events as a young boy.

21
01:41:20,869 --> 01:41:26,869
Strode's opening narration, documenting early black settlers, continues his onscreen naming
of individuals overlooked by history from the 1972 documentary Black Rodeo.

22
01:46:10,434 --> 01:46:16,434
The film explores themes of shootout, racial tension, gun fight.
